        "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Domain of a Hyperboloid",
        "problem": "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$",
        "worked_solution": "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1 & =\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & =9((x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1) \\\\ z & =\\pm 3\\sqrt{(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the ellipse $(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1,$ which includes the boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\ge 1}.\n$$",
