      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-74903",
        "knowledge_point_id": "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed",
        "knowledge_point": "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions",
        "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=$",
        "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {0}^{2}}{{0}^{3}+0\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2{y}^{2}x+y{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$",
