        "knowledge_point": "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid",
        "problem": "Given the elliptic paraboloid $z+4=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4}$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis.",
        "worked_solution": "To find the $x$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+4 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-4)^{2}}{4} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{16}{4} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=0$\n\nTherefore, the intercept with the $x$-axis is $(0,0,0)$ only.",
