    {
      "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14099076",
      "id": "q-164269",
      "status": "supported",
      "reason": null,
      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-164269",
        "knowledge_point_id": "ddf82ff9-ea7c-5f7f-ac18-07f8e75b717e",
        "knowledge_point": "Identifying Properties of an Elliptic Paraboloid",
        "problem": "Consider the elliptic paraboloid $x=\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{6}.$ Which of the following gives its axis of symmetry, written as an intersection of two planes?",
        "worked_solution": "First, we write our equation as \n\n$$\nx-0=\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z-(-1))^{2}}{6}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $x$-axis that has its vertex at $(0,3,-1).$\n Since it is oriented along the $x$-axis, we conclude that its axis of symmetry is parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nThe axis of symmetry should pass through the vertex of the paraboloid. Therefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry can be written as an intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=3 \\\\ z=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices_complete": true,
            "choices": [
